Rewind states without the emulated memories, and a compact page table

A raw state (patch 0014) still copies the 296 MB of a New 3DS game's memories
through the archive at every snapshot: 136 ms on the host, a visible stop in
play every few seconds. Those memories are four flat arrays (VRAM, FCRAM, the
New 3DS's extra RAM, the DSP's RAM), and a rewind can keep its own copy of
them and compare it with the live arrays away from the emulation thread.

This adds MemorySystem::GetRegions (where the four live) and
SetRegionsOutsideArchive (while set, the memory archive writes and reads each
of them as empty), and a `memory_outside` flag and a `regions` out-parameter to
retro_ctr_rewind_capture / _restore: the state is taken and loaded without the
four, and the frontend learns where they are (after a restore, the new ones),
writes its copy back into them before the next frame, and so restores the
whole console. The load-time hooks that look at guest memory only take
pointers into it (PicaCore::CommandList, the page tables), which stay valid
when the bytes are written afterwards.

Without the memories a state still cost 130 ms, nearly all of it the first CPU
core archiving the page table: one MemoryRef per page, 1 << 20 of them, each a
tracked shared_ptr. In the same rewind mode (PageTable::SetCompactArchive) a
page table is archived as runs of pages with one attribute that point into one
backing at consecutive offsets (or into none): 23 runs for a homebrew, each
backing still archived through the archive, so its identity is kept. Save
states keep the format every build reads.

Measured on the host (Tools/3ds-bench rewind, Craftus Reloaded): the core's
part of a snapshot 130 ms, then 12.4 ms; a snapshot restored and played 300
frames sounds identical to the full state of the same moment restored the
usual way (one picture frame differs, the first after the renderer is rebuilt,
as after any load).

